          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"flaubert-madame-bovary-fc563b7babfd\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":1,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.93,\"analysis\":\"“Tu sais bien que je t’aime” explicitly presents the speaker’s love as an already established and supposedly known emotional state. Its urgent delivery adds limited exclamatory force, but the clear avowal remains dominant. It reassures rather than undertaking a new commitment.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The exclamation marks and crisis-like exchange give the avowal some emotionally triggered force, but not enough to weaken its clear propositional content.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"A character directly tells another character, “Tu sais bien que je t’aime,” within spoken dialogue.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"The speaker is astonished by the addressee’s distress and repeatedly urges them to calm down. The love avowal functions as immediate reassurance and as an appeal to something the addressee is said already to know; the following “viens!” reinforces the urgency. Nothing in the supplied excerpt establishes a distinct promise or relational undertaking, regardless of the speaker’s possible sincerity outside this context.\" ,\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Tu sais bien que je t’aime!”\",\"supports\":\"The complement “que je t’aime” clearly avows a loving state, while “tu sais bien” frames that state as established and already known, strongly supporting T.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Qu’as-tu? qu’as-tu? répétait-il stupéfait. Calme-toi! reprends-toi!”\",\"supports\":\"The repeated questions, astonishment, and urgent imperatives establish an emotionally charged situation, giving limited support to E and showing the reassuring function of the avowal.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The avowal is followed by “viens!”\",\"supports\":\"The adjacent imperative confirms urgency, but because it is grammatically separate from the love statement, it does not by itself turn that statement into a commitment or undertaking.\",\"confidence\":0.95},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is extracted from Gustave Flaubert’s French novel Madame Bovary and matched as “je t’aime.”\",\"supports\":\"Confirms the language, work, and identified core expression without adding unsupported plot assumptions.\",\"confidence\":0.99}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"moderate\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T captures the explicit avowal, while a low E score captures its urgent emotional delivery. P is unnecessary because reassurance and an adjacent command do not establish undertaking force, and no residual core feature requires O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
      ],
